# EMCOMM Field Node — Pre-Deployment Checklist

Items that cannot be verified by the provisioner and must be confirmed against
real hardware and your group's plan before a node is considered deployable.

## 1. Callsign and Licence
The callsign entered at provisioning is written into the JS8Call profile as MyCall
and into the Direwolf config as MYCALL.

Confirm:
    - the callsign is real, current, and belongs to the operator of THIS node
    - the operator holds the class of licence required for the modes enabled
    - no node ships with a placeholder still in place

Verify with:
    grep -i mycall ~/.config/JS8Call.ini ~/.config/direwolf/direwolf.conf

## 2. Profile Contamination (configs/*.ini, configs/*.conf)
A profile captured from a working install carries that machine's identity.
Before distributing any profile, check for:
    - a real callsign         (MyCall / MYCALL)
    - a grid square           (MyGrid)
    - absolute home paths     (/home/<someone>/...)
    - hardcoded audio devices (a specific card or PCI path)

Verify with:
    grep -nE "MyCall|MyGrid|/home/|alsa_|plughw|pci-" configs/*.ini configs/*.conf

Replace identity with MYCALL_PLACEHOLDER, paths with HOME_PLACEHOLDER, and leave
grid and audio device fields EMPTY for the operator to set per node.

## 3. Audio Interface (JS8Call, Direwolf)
Sound-card interface device names differ per machine and per adapter, and USB
audio enumeration order is not stable across reboots.

Verify with the interface connected:
    arecord -l
    pactl list short sinks
    pactl list short sources

Set the result in JS8Call -> File -> Settings -> Audio, and in ADEVICE in
direwolf.conf. Consider a udev rule to pin a stable name if the index moves.

## 4. PTT Keying (Direwolf)
The shipped Direwolf config uses PTT CM108, which is common for USB sound-card
interfaces but has documented reliability issues on Linux.

Confirm PTT actually keys the radio, into a dummy load, before relying on it.
If CM108 fails, a serial RTS/DTR PTT interface is the usual fallback.

## 5. Operating Area Map Tiles (scripts/fetch_map_tiles.py)
The tile fetcher needs a bounding box for YOUR operating area. Do not ship one
group's area to another group, and do not commit an area you would rather not
publish.

Confirm:
    - the bounding box covers your actual response area
    - the zoom range is deep enough to be useful but small enough to fit on disk
    - tiles land under ~/EMCOMM_Data/Offline_Maps/Offline_Tiles/

## 6. Time Sync Behaviour
Digital modes are timing-sensitive; JS8Call in particular needs the clock close
to correct. The dock-trigger autostart waits up to 15s for chrony to report a
normal leap status, then continues regardless.

Verify actual cold-start sync time:
    chronyc tracking
Adjust the wait ceiling if your GPS takes longer to acquire.

## 7. Radio Interface Enumeration Timing
Autostart waits up to 10s for /dev/ttyUSB* or /dev/ttyACM* before launching the
digital-mode applications. Confirm your interface enumerates within that window
after a cold boot, or raise the ceiling.

## 8. Dock Trigger (optional — skip if you have no dock)
The udev rule ships with a PLACEHOLDER vendor/product ID. It will not match your
hardware as-is.

Find yours:
    udevadm monitor --environment --udev
(then dock/undock and read the actual idVendor/idProduct)

Update 99-dock-trigger.rules, or leave this step unchecked entirely.

## 9. Meshtastic Net Parameters
Region, preset and channel must match across every node in the group or they
will not hear each other. See the staged reference at
~/EMCOMM_Data/Meshtastic/meshtastic_setup.md.

Confirm the group has agreed region, preset and channel name before deployment,
and decide per node whether position reporting should be on.

## 10. Offline Knowledgebase Currency
The Kiwix ZIM download is pinned to a specific dated file and checksum. That URL
will eventually 404 as upstream rotates snapshots.

If the download or checksum fails, pick a current file from
https://download.kiwix.org/zim/ and update the URL and SHA-256 in the script.

## 11. Unattended Updates
If the "appliance build" step was run, unattended updates are disabled. That is
correct for an imaged node kept offline, and wrong for a laptop in daily use.

Either way: patch the node manually before each deployment cycle.

## 12. Full Dry Run
Before the node counts as ready, exercise it end to end:
    - transmit and receive on each enabled mode, into a dummy load or on a net
    - confirm maps render offline with the network cable pulled
    - confirm the document server answers at http://127.0.0.1:8085
    - run the machine on battery for the duration of an expected shift
